    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Madurai was the capital of the Pandyan dynasty from the 7th century CE, and it passed in turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, the Madurai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1801.</li><li>The city was built around its temples, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> redesigned it as concentric quadrangular streets according to the Shilpa Shastras, a plan that the British kept when they demolished the fortifications in 1837.</li><li>Madurai was plundered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> in 1311, fought over in the Carnatic wars and besieged by the English in 1763 and 1764, and in the 20th century it became a centre of the independence and temple entry movements.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to the Tamil tradition of the third Sangam, the merit of a poet's work was tested by setting it afloat in the lotus tank of the temple: it was believed that a divine force would make the work of superior merit float and the inferior ones sink.</p></aside>
    </section>

